==English==
{{webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
* From Latin ''[[constitutum]]'', past participle of [[constituo|constituere]]. Constructed from the prefix [[con-]] and {{term|statuere||to place, set}}.

===Verb===
{{en-verb|constitut|ing}}

# To cause to stand; to [[establish]]; to [[enact]].
#: Laws appointed and constituted by lawful authority.-- [[w:Jeremy Taylor|Jeremy Taylor]].
# To [[make up]]; to [[compose]]; to [[form]].
#: Truth and reason constitute that intellectual gold that defies destruction.--Johnson.
# To [[appoint]], depute, or elect to an office; to make and empower.
#: Me didst Thou constitute a priest of thine.-- [[w:William Wordsworth|William Wordsworth]].

====Synonyms====
* [[establish]], [[enact]]
* [[make up]], [[compose]], [[form]]

====Related terms====
* [[constituent]]
* [[constituency]]
* [[constitution]]
* [[constitutional]]
* [[constitutionalization]]
* [[constitutive]]

